= Tutored Lessons
Apache Incubator PMC
2026-08-22
:jbake-type: guide
:jbake-status: published
:idprefix:
:toc:
:imagesdir: ../images/

== Tutored Lessons

Each lesson is an interactive session with an AI tutor rather than a document
to read. You paste the lesson into a chat model, and it teaches you one idea at
a time, sets exercises, waits for your answer before giving its own, and
finishes with a self-check.

The lessons are written and fact-checked against ASF policy, the Incubator's
guides and the Incubator wiki, and they are explicit about which of those a
given statement comes from.

== How to run one

. Find the lesson you want below and click *Copy prompt*.
. Open any capable chat model.
. Paste the prompt as the first message, or into a system prompt field if the
tool has one.
. Say hello. The tutor takes it from there.

Nothing is installed and nothing is recorded. If you prefer, *View the prompt*
opens the plain text so you can read it first or copy it by hand.

== Getting Started

These three come first. They are the prerequisite for everything below, and
they assume no prior knowledge of the ASF.

[[lesson-1]]
=== 1. What the Incubator is, and whether you want it

What a podling is, what a project hands over and gets back, who does what, and
whether a community is ready to propose.

link:lessons/lesson-01.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-01.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-2]]
=== 2. The Apache Way, in practice

Consensus, merit, community over code, transparency and independence, and how to
tell whether a decision was made the Apache way.

link:lessons/lesson-02.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-02.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-3]]
=== 3. Working in public: lists, tone, culture, time zones

Why messages read as rude when nobody meant it, choosing the right channel,
working across time zones, and what the code of conduct asks.

link:lessons/lesson-03.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-03.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

== Podling Startup and the PPMC

For people running a podling day to day. These five can be taken in any order.

[[lesson-4]]
=== 4. From proposal to podling

The path from idea to podling: what a champion does, what a proposal has to
establish, credible initial committers, and honest known risks.

link:lessons/lesson-04.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-04.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-5]]
=== 5. Getting set up with Infra

What Infra does and what the project does, choosing between self-serve, a ticket
and Whimsy, and writing a request that needs no round trip.

link:lessons/lesson-05.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-05.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-6]]
=== 6. How decisions get made: lazy consensus, votes, vetoes

When something needs no vote, consensus or a formal vote, who is binding, the
two-stage release vote, and what makes a veto valid.

link:lessons/lesson-06.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-06.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-7]]
=== 7. Growing committers and the PPMC

What committers and PPMC members can and cannot do, running each process end to
end, who to invite, and the awkward cases.

link:lessons/lesson-07.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-07.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-8]]
=== 8. Writing a report the IPMC can use

Who reads a podling report and what they look for, what belongs in each section,
reporting a bad month honestly, and the submission mechanics.

link:lessons/lesson-08.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-08.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

== Legal, Branding and IP

The rules in these three come from the Foundation rather than the Incubator,
so they apply the same way to a top-level project.

[[lesson-9]]
=== 9. Legal basics: licences, ICLAs, provenance

Who owns contributed code, who files an ICLA or a software grant, what may be
included in a release, and when incoming code needs IP clearance.

link:lessons/lesson-09.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-09.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-10]]
=== 10. Names, trademarks and branding

What a podling must call itself, who owns the name, the suitable name search,
what a website has to carry, and what third parties may do.

link:lessons/lesson-10.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-10.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-11]]
=== 11. Privacy and data handling

Analytics, cookies, third-party assets and embeds, the public mailing list
record and removal requests, and handling personal data.

link:lessons/lesson-11.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-11.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

== Releases

Track D is the whole of a podling release, from what the artifact has to be to
getting it announced without a link nobody can take back.

[[lesson-12]]
=== 12. Anatomy of a podling release

What counts as a release, what a source package must contain, signing and
checksums, where artifacts live, and what is different for a podling.

link:lessons/lesson-12.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-12.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-13]]
=== 13. Running a release vote

The two stages and what travels between them, calling a vote reviewers can act
on, the arithmetic, answering a -1, and closing the vote.

link:lessons/lesson-13.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-13.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

[[lesson-14]]
=== 14. Announcing and distributing

What happens between a passed vote and an announcement, what a download page
must contain, what an announcement must link, and correcting what is out.

link:lessons/lesson-14.md[View the prompt] |
link:lessons/lesson-14.md[Copy prompt, role="copy-lesson"]

== As an installable skill

Some AI tools can install a skill, which saves the pasting. The fourteen lessons
build into one, so `/incubator-lesson list` shows the catalogue and
`/incubator-lesson 6` starts that lesson.

Build it from a checkout with `python3 tools/ai-tutor/build-skill.py`, then
install the result the way your tool expects. The lesson text inside it is
copied from the same files this page publishes, so both routes run the same
tutor.

== A caution

These are teaching prompts, not policy. Where a lesson and an ASF policy page
disagree, the policy page is correct. Models also make mistakes, so if you need
a definitive answer for a real decision, ask your mentors or the Incubator's
pass:[<a href="https://lists.apache.org/list.html?general@incubator.apache.org">general mailing list</a>].

Corrections are welcome on that list. The lessons live in
link:https://github.com/apache/incubator/tree/master/tools/ai-tutor[tools/ai-tutor]
in the website repository.

++++
<script>
(function () {
  function flash(el, text) {
    var original = el.textContent;
    el.textContent = text;
    setTimeout(function () { el.textContent = original; }, 2000);
  }
  document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
    var link = event.target.closest ? event.target.closest('a.copy-lesson') : null;
    if (!link) { return; }
    event.preventDefault();
    var url = link.getAttribute('href');
    fetch(url).then(function (response) {
      if (!response.ok) { throw new Error(response.status); }
      return response.text();
    }).then(function (text) {
      return navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
    }).then(function () {
      flash(link, 'Copied');
    }).catch(function () {
      // Clipboard access can be blocked, and older browsers lack it entirely.
      // Send the reader to the file rather than leaving them with nothing.
      window.location.href = url;
    });
  });
})();
</script>
++++
